    <statement><p>The slope field for the equation <m>y'=-x+y</m> is shown below</p>
<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap11Sec2__Q03-image-1.png" width="41%"/>
<p>On a print out of this slope field, sketch the solutions that pass
through the points</p>
<p><alert>(i)</alert> (0,0);</p>
<p><alert>(ii)</alert> (-3,1); and</p>
<p><alert>(iii)</alert> (-1,0).</p>
<p>From your sketch, what is the equation of the solution to the
differential equation that passes through (-1,0)?
(Verify that your solution is correct by substituting it into the
differential equation.)</p>
<p><m>y =</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>x+1</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap11Sec2__Q03-image-2.png" width="41%"/>




We can guess from this that the solution through (-1,0)
is <m>y = x+1</m>.  Plugging this into the differential equation
confirms this.



</solution>
